  data:  # 653e
    kind: invariant  # 653e
    title: "Object creation has no agreed edge representation: ruby emits calls-to-initialize and never instantiates, while 8 analyzers emit instantiates"  # 653e
    status: violated  # 653e
    priority: 2  # 653e
    parent:  # 653e
    tags:  # 653e
    - analyzer  # 653e
    - classification_axes_audit  # 653e
    - concept_audit  # 653e
    isbefore: []  # 653e
    duplicate_of: []  # 653e
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 653e
    pr_ref:  # 653e
    description: "Found by the Fundamental Concept Audit of `edge.meta['call_construct']`\n(2026-08-20), while adjudicating why 5 sites carry `call_construct` on an\n`instantiates` edge. The call_construct question turned out to be downstream\nof this one.\n\n**The disagreement, measured across the tree:**\n\n    ruby.py:2263        Klass.new(...) -> resolves to `Klass#initialize`\n                        edge_type=\"calls\", dst = the initialize METHOD\n                        ruby emits `instantiates` ZERO times, anywhere\n\n    dart.py:1019,1060   edge_type=\"instantiates\", dst = the class\n    csharp.py:1656,     edge_type=\"instantiates\", dst = the class /\n      1668,1683           (post-WI-fagit) the constructor symbol\n    java.py (2 sites)   edge_type=\"instantiates\"\n    py.py (3 sites)     edge_type=\"instantiates\"\n    js_ts.py (2)        edge_type=\"instantiates\"\n    php.py (1)          edge_type=\"instantiates\"\n    cpp.py (4)          edge_type=\"instantiates\"\n    verilog.py (1)      edge_type=\"instantiates\"\n\nEight analyzers emit `instantiates`; ruby never does. No linker mints\n`instantiates` either (checked: the only two hits under `linkers/` are a\nconsumer and a docstring), so there is no cross-language backstop.\n\n**Why each analyzer is locally correct, and the defect is therefore upstream.**\nUnder ADR-0023 `edge_type` names the RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN src AND dst. Ruby's\nedge points at `Klass#initialize`, and the relationship to an initialize method\ngenuinely is \"calls\". Dart/C#'s edge points at the class or constructor, and the\nrelationship to a class genuinely is \"instantiates\". Neither analyzer is\nmislabelling its own edge. **They disagree about what the dst of an\nobject-creation edge should be**, and `edge_type` co-varies with that choice.\nSo this cannot be fixed by relabelling edges; it needs a ruling on the dst.\n\n**A consumer is already absorbing the split.** `linkers/method_call_recovery.py`:\n\n    # fallback ``calls`` (Java/Kotlin/Python) or an explicit ``instantiates``\n    _CLASS_HINT_EDGE_TYPES = frozenset({\"calls\", \"instantiates\"})\n\nThat is the audit playbook's Step 4 signature symptom (\"consumers maintain\nhardcoded sets\"). Note the comment is ALSO stale: it names Java and Python as\n`calls`-fallback languages, but `java.py` and `py.py` both emit `instantiates`\ntoday. A set held to absorb a producer disagreement, with a comment that no\nlonger describes the producers, is the shape this repo has filed before.\n\n**Consequence.** `hypergumbo survey` filtered on `type == \"instantiates\"`\nreturns Dart/C#/Java/Python/JS/PHP/C++ object creation and SILENTLY OMITS every\nRuby object creation. A call-graph consumer sees the mirror image: Ruby's\nconstructor invocations appear as calls, Dart's do not.\n\n**Structural sibling.** This is the INV-nosoz shape one axis over — one\nrelationship, several labels, split by language. INV-nosoz is five vocabularies\nfor \"inheritance edge\"; this is two for \"object creation\".\n\n**NOT a duplicate of WI-nopod** (constructor/factory BINDINGS for\nconstruction-keyed framework patterns, done) — that is about recording the\nbinding, this is about which edge_type and dst the construction edge takes.\n\n**Open question needing a ruling before a fix:** should an object-creation edge\npoint at the class (relationship `instantiates`) or at the initializer\n(relationship `calls`)? Ruby's choice carries real information — it resolves to\nthe actual callee body, which is what the call graph wants. Dart/C#'s choice is\nwhat a dependency consumer wants. A third option is BOTH edges. Rule this\nbefore touching any analyzer; relabelling one side without the ruling just\nmoves the inconsistency."  # 653e
    fields:  # 653e
      statement: "One source-language construct (object creation) must produce one relationship label across languages, so a consumer can enumerate every object creation with a single edge_type query."  # 653e
      root_cause: "The analyzers disagree about the dst of an object-creation edge, and edge_type co-varies with that choice. ruby.py:2263 resolves Klass.new to Klass#initialize and emits edge_type=calls with dst = the initialize METHOD; dart/csharp/java/py/js_ts/php/cpp/verilog emit edge_type=instantiates with dst = the class or constructor symbol. Each label is locally correct under ADR-0023 for the dst its own analyzer chose, so the defect is upstream of edge_type, in the dst choice. ruby emits instantiates zero times and no linker mints it, so there is no cross-language backstop."  # 653e
